💡 **SUGGESTION: validate the createIssue response before pointing `defered` tags at it**

`fileDigest` accepts whatever the bridge returns:

```javascript
var created = t.createIssue(issue.title, issue.body);
out.digest = created && created.number;
```

If the payload parses but carries no `number` (bridge error body, schema drift), `out.digest` is `undefined` and every counted thread is resolved with `defered: #undefined` (js/common/mergeBackstop.js:547) — the merge then proceeds with machine-closed threads whose deferral pointer is garbage, silently. That skirts the module's own invariant ("never silently merge without a digest") and diverges from the repo's precedent: `js/common/trackers.js:884` treats "returned no issue id" as a hard error for exactly this reason.

One line aligns it with the fail-closed contract:

```javascript
if (!created || !created.number) {
    throw new Error('mergeBackstop: digest filing returned no issue number — merge deferred');
}
```

Low likelihood (the bridge normally throws on failure), hence suggestion rather than important.

🪶 **NIT: `normalizedIo` mutates the injected io bag**

```javascript
IO_KEYS.forEach(function (k) {
    if (typeof b[k] !== 'function') b[k] = null;
});
```

The module header advertises "Pure module — no dmtools globals (the io is injected)", but normalization writes `null` slots back onto the caller's object. Production callers pass a fresh `githubBridgeIo()` each tick, so it is invisible there; only a shared or frozen io bag (a frozen object throws in this `'use strict'` module — the merge would defer with a TypeError) would notice.

Purely API hygiene: normalize into a local object (`var out = {}; ... out[k] = ...`) to keep the injected bag untouched.

🪶 **NIT: unmarked/NIT human threads render an unlabeled severity in the digest's human table**

`severityLabel` maps only `BLOCKING`/`IMPORTANT`; everything else falls through to the raw class:

```javascript
var SEVERITY_LABELS = { BLOCKING: '🚨 BLOCKING', IMPORTANT: '⚠️ IMPORTANT' };
function severityLabel(severityClass) {
    return SEVERITY_LABELS[severityClass] || severityClass;
}
```

A human thread with no recognizable severity marker classifies `NONE` → `severityClassOf` → `SUGGESTION` (js/common/mergeBackstop.js:105-108), so the `open (human)` table shows a bare `SUGGESTION` string with no emoji — inconsistent with the labeled rows above it. Cosmetic only (the classification itself is correct: unmarked stays below the backstop threshold); adding `SUGGESTION: '💡 SUGGESTION'` and `NIT: '🪶 NIT'` to the label map would keep the table uniform.

